00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:54.860
Take it apart, and the MX-1 places risk-5 cores directly alongside DDR5 and SSD arrays

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.720
connected through CXL links. This layout lets the cores perform local reductions in filtering

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.940
before any data crosses the host PCIe bus. Each CXL attached module therefore cuts the volume

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:14.100
of traffic that would otherwise traverse the narrower host interface. The design also lets the SSDs

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:20.180
serve as a slower but larger tier that the risk 5 cores can address without first staging everything

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:27.040
and host DRAM. When the working set fits inside the pool DDR5, latency stays comparable to local

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:33.620
memory. Once the set spills to the SSD tier, bandwidth drops but capacity scales without adding

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:39.240
more host sockets. The trade-off flips when the ratio of compute to data movement exceeds the

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:45.660
CXL link rate. At that point, the added core is no longer offset the extra hop. On the horizon,

